es and reduces hidden tiles, making it harder to form high-point hand. No clear gain. Best to pass and wait for better draws.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nMy hand is not close to a winning shape yet, and making an exposed Chow now reduces hand flexibility for little immediate benefit. Keeping the hand concealed and waiting to improve sequences is better strategically. Passing avoids committing to a weak Chow that doesn\u2019t advance toward a scoring pattern.  \n\n**Answer:** Pass"}
