. Could get pung.  \n\nCheck **potential waits**: if I keep B7 pair, W2 W3 W5 W6 B2 B3 need to form two chows:  \nOption 1: W2 W3 + W4 (draw) and W5 W6 + W7 (draw) \u2014 two chows in W suit. Remaining B2 B3 + B4 (draw) for third chow (but B4 already in exposed meld \u2014 can\u2019t reuse same tile? Wait, exposed meld uses B2 B3 B4, but in hand I have B2 B3, so B4 would make another B2 B3 B4 chow, is that allowed? Yes, you can have multiple identical chows.  \n\nSo possible final hand:  \n- Exposed: B2 B3 B4  \n- Concealed: T2 T3 T4, B2 B3 B4 (new), W2 W3 W4, W5 W6 W7, pair B7.  \n\nThat\u2019s 4 chows + 1 pair, All Simples, All Chows, Short Straight in W (W2-W4 and W5-W7 are not consecutive sequences? Actually W2-W4 and W5-W7 are not overlapping, so not Short Straight. But Mixed Double Chow between T2T3T4 and B2B3B4 (same numbers) \u2014 1 point. Also possible Pure Double Chow between the two B2B3B4 chows \u2014 1 point. Could reach 8 points: All Chows (2) + All Simples (2) + Pure Double Chow (1) + Mixed Double Chow (1) + Short Straight (1?) possibly.  \n\nBut that requires drawing W4, W7, B4 \u2014 feasible.  \n\nThus, I should keep tiles that help these sequences: keep W2, W3, W5, W6, B2, B3, T2, T3, T4, B7\u00d72.  \n\nThe weakest tile in hand for this plan is **W2 or W3**? No, they are needed.  \n\nActually, looking again: I have 10 tiles: W2, W3, W5, W6, T2, T3, T4, B2, B3, B7\u00d72. That\u2019s 11 tiles? Let\u2019s count: W2(1), W3(1), W5(1), W6(1), T2(1), T3(1), T4(1), B2(1), B3(1), B7(2) \u2192 total 10 indeed.  \n\nFor melds: T2 T3 T4 is one chow; need two more chows from the remaining 7 tiles (W2,W3,W5,W6,B2,B3,B7).  \n\nIf I aim for two chows from W: W2 W3 + W4, and W5 W6 + W7, that uses W2,W3,W5,W6. Remaining B2,B3,B7\u00d72 could form: B7 pair for final pair, B2 B3 + B4 for chow \u2014 but then B7 leftover one? That\u2019s 14 tiles:  \nMeld1: B2 B3 B4 (exposed)  \nMeld2: T2 T3 T4 (concealed)  \nMeld3: B2 B3 B4 (concealed) \u2014 uses B2 B3 B4 again  \nMeld4: W2 W3 W4  \nPair: B7 B7  \n\nThat\u2019s valid: but I have B2,B3 only one each in hand, so need B4 for chow.  \n\nBut if I get B4 for chow, then B7 pair remains. But then total tiles:  \nHand: W2,W3,W5,W6,B2,B3,B7,B7,T2,T3,T4 \u2192 plus exposed meld B2,B3,B4 \u2192 total 14 when I draw W4,W7,B4 and discard nothing? That\u2019s 3 draws, I must discard after each draw, so must discard some tiles.  \n\nGiven complexity, the safest discard now is one that doesn\u2019t hurt potential but is not part of a strong sequence pair. **T4** is already part of a complete chow, but keeping it locks that chow. But if I discard T4, I free up a slot but lose that chow. Given T4 has 3 left, I could recover later if needed.  \n\nBut maybe discard **W2**? W2 has 4 left unseen, W2 is useful with W3.  \n\nBetter to discard the more isolated tile: **B7** \u2014 it\u2019s a pair now, but forming a pung is unlikely (only 2 left), and having a pair early might limit flexibility. Discarding one B7 leaves one B7, which could later become a pair with a drawn B7, or part of a chow B6 B7 B8 (B8 mostly gone) or B5 B6 B7.  \n\nGiven B7 chow possibilities: B5 B6 B7 (B5 4 left, B6 3 left) or B6 B7 B8 (B8 1 left) \u2014 possible. So keeping one B7 is fine.  \n\nBut to simplify, discard a tile that is less useful for chow forming. **W6** is less useful without W5 and W7 \u2014 but W5 is in hand, W7 is available (4 left), so W5 W6 W7 is possible.  \n\nActually check all valid actions: Play B7, W2, B3, B2, T4, W3, T2, W5, W6, T3.  \nAmong these, the ones that break a chow in progress:  \n- T2,T3,T4 chow \u2014 discard any breaks it.  \n- W2,W3 \u2014 discard breaks.  \n- B2,B3 \u2014 discard breaks possible chow with B4.  \n\nSo best is to discard a tile not in any potential chow pair: but all are in some potential.  \n\nGiven I want to keep All Simples possibility, discard **B7** is safe (B7 is simple). But it\u2019s a pair.  \n\nLet\u2019s see opponents\u2019 discards: Previous player played T4,T5,F2 \u2014 T4 might be safe. Next player played T2,T9,F3 etc. T2 might be dangerous.  \n\nBut since we\u2019re early, I\u2019ll choose discard that keeps W suit sequences and B sequences:  \n\n**W5** is somewhat isolated without W4 or W7 yet, but W5 W6 can become chow with W4 or W7. But W5 with W6 is good.  \n\nActually, looking at hand: W2 W3 (needs W1 or W4), W5 W6 (needs W4 or W7) \u2014 they both need W4, which could be a conflict. So maybe discard W5 to focus on one sequence (W2 W3 W4) and use other suit for another chow.  \n\n**Decision:** I\u2019ll discard **W5** because it competes for W4 with W2 W3, and keeping W2 W3 W4 chow + using B for other chows is smoother.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nDiscarding W5 preserves W2-W3 chow potential, keeps B2-B3 for possible chow with B4, keeps T2-T3-T4 chow intact, and doesn\u2019t break the B7 pair. This maintains flexibility toward All Chows + All Simples, which can reach 8 points with additional patterns like Short Straight or Mixed Double Chow.  \n\n**Answer:** Play W5"}
