I can think T2 T3 T4 is chow, leaving T4 and W6.  \nSo after using T2 T3 T4 as a chow, hand left: T4, W6 \u2192 need one more chow and a pair.  \nPossible:  \n- Chow W4W5W6 needs W4,W5,W6 (I have W6).  \n- Chow W5W6W7 needs W5,W7.  \n- Pair from T4 or W6.  \n\nSo still far.  \n\nJ3 is an honor, hard to complete meld. Better discard now.  \n\nAlso check prevailing/seat wind: F4 is North, I don't have F4 in hand.  \n\nThus discard J3 seems reasonable to simplify hand and aim for All Simples + possible All Chows.  \n\nOther options:  \nDiscard W6 \u2192 keep J3 aiming for Dragon Pung (2 pts) but needs 2 more J3 (2 left). Possible but less likely.  \n\nDiscard T4 \u2192 keep two suits, but T4 is useful for T2T3T4 chow.  \n\nSo best: discard J3.  \n\n**But** the problem says my previous invalid answer was \"Play J3\", maybe that action is invalid now due to game rules? Possibly because it's not my turn to play after a chi? But it says valid actions include Play J3, so it should be valid.  \n\nGiven valid actions list: Play W2; Play J3; Play T4; Play W3; Play T2; Play W6; Play T3.  \n\nHold on \u2014 why is W3 in valid actions if my meld used W3? Maybe the meld W2W3W4 is NOT using my hand tiles? Possibly the meld is from someone else's discard and my hand still has W3? But my hand tiles list [W2:1; W3:1; ...] \u2014 that's *before* meld inclusion? Actually melds are separate. The hand tiles list given might be the total tiles in hand including melds? Usually in MCR state, hand tiles are what you physically hold, melds are separate exposed sets. So if W2,W3 are in meld, they shouldn't be in hand.  \n\nBut here hand tiles list includes W2:1, W3:1 \u2014 maybe means those are still in hand, and the meld [W2,W3,W4] is an **exposed meld from earlier** that used W2,W3 from hand then. So maybe W2 and W3 are no longer in hand, but state's hand list is **before forming meld**? That doesn't make sense because history shows I already Chi W3 earlier.  \n\nActually checking my history